## 1. The Strategy: Why These Patterns

### `<title>` tag — Pattern
```
"{Thematic Title}" — Charles Spurgeon Sermon No. {N} | SpurgeonTV
```

**Why this works:**
- The **thematic title in quotes** matches how users search: *"Compel Them to Come In" Spurgeon*
- **"Charles Spurgeon"** is the #1 keyword for this niche — always included
- **"Sermon No. {N}"** targets researchers and scholars who cite Spurgeon by number
- **"SpurgeonTV"** builds brand recognition in SERPs
- Kept under **~85 chars** so Google doesn't truncate

### `<meta description>` — Pattern
```
{Scripture Reference} — Charles H. Spurgeon's famous sermon "{Thematic Title}". {First meaningful sentence of the sermon…}
```

**Why this works:**
- The **scripture reference** (e.g., `Malachi 3:6`) appears in countless search queries
- **"famous sermon"** triggers informational intent — the user is looking to read, not buy
- The **first real sentence** of the sermon provides fresh, unique, non-repetitive content on every page
- Always trimmed to **≤ 160 characters** — Google's display limit

### `keywords` meta tag
Even though Google doesn't use `<meta keywords>` for ranking, Bing, Yahoo, and AdSense **do** use them for contextual ad matching. Keywords added:

```
Charles Spurgeon, Charles H. Spurgeon, Spurgeon sermon, Spurgeon sermons,
{Thematic Title}, Sermon {N}, Volume {V},
{Scripture Reference}, classic sermons, Reformed preaching,
Victorian preaching, Prince of Preachers, Metropolitan Tabernacle, SpurgeonTV
```

